## Monorepo Structure

apps/
  cli/
  server/
  dashboard/

packages/
  shared/
    event-bus/
    storage/
    traffic/
    replay/
    types/
    utils/

Dashboard is a standalone Next.js application.

Dashboard communicates with the server only through REST and WebSocket APIs.

The dashboard must never import server internals.

---

## Design Rules

Every feature must:

- expose a public interface
- be independently testable
- be independently replaceable
- avoid global mutable state
- avoid circular dependencies

No module may directly access another module's internal implementation.

Use dependency injection or events instead.

---

## Storage Rules

Never store application data directly inside services.

Always depend on a StorageProvider interface.

The first implementation is in-memory storage.

Future implementations may include SQLite, PostgreSQL, Redis, or cloud storage.

No business logic should require changes when switching storage backends.

---

## Event Rules

Use the shared EventBus package.

Emit strongly typed events.

Never emit stringly typed events.

Every event must include:

- event id
- timestamp
- tunnel id (if applicable)
- correlation/request id (if applicable)

Events should be immutable.

---

## API Rules

REST APIs should be versionable.

Prefer:

/api/v1/...

Responses must use consistent DTOs.

Do not expose internal entities directly.

---

## Testing Requirements

Every feature requires:

- unit tests
- integration tests
- documentation

Before completion verify:

- TypeScript
- ESLint
- Prettier
- Existing unit tests
- Existing E2E tests
- New tests

No regressions are acceptable.

---

## Performance

Avoid unnecessary allocations.

Avoid blocking the event loop.

Support thousands of requests without retaining unnecessary memory.

Large request/response bodies should be handled efficiently.

---

## Documentation

Every new module must include:

- architecture overview
- dependency graph
- folder structure
- public interfaces
- extension points
- examples

---

## Forbidden

Do not rewrite Layer 1 networking.

Do not change the tunnel protocol.

Do not change CLI behavior unless explicitly requested.

Do not tightly couple the dashboard to the server.

Do not implement future roadmap features unless requested.